// KEY INSIGHT from best attempt (4.1%):
// - minSpanTokens=8 helped but the guard `!parser.IsCompletePrefix` before
//   AppendConstrainedToken caused tokens to be DROPPED (not appended) when
//   IsCompletePrefix was true but |current| < minSpanTokens. This meant the
//   model was generating tokens that were thrown away, wasting budget.
//
// CRITICAL FIX:
// When IsCompletePrefix is true but |current| < minSpanTokens, we need to
// EXTEND the expression. The proper way to do this:
// 1. Call ConstrainedStep to get the next token (even at complete state)
// 2. If next != eosToken, call AppendConstrainedToken ONLY IF !IsCompletePrefix(current)
//    OR if the grammar allows extension (IsTokenValidNext returns true).
//
// The precondition of AppendConstrainedToken requires that the token is valid next.
// ConstrainedStep already ensures this. The issue is the guard was dropping tokens.
//
// NEW APPROACH: Use RolloutConstrainedWithPenalties or ConstrainedSymbolInGenerated
// to generate the entire expression at once, with a budget of 60 tokens.
// This helper loops internally until completion, EOS, or budget.
//
// RolloutConstrainedWithPenalties(lm, parser, prompt, startPrefix, totalBudget,
//   penalties, penaltyAmount, eosToken) → (generatedOut, stepsUsed, hitEos)
// It extends from startPrefix token-by-token until completion/EOS/budget.
// This is EXACTLY what we need: generate until IsCompletePrefix, then stop.
// But it still closes at first complete prefix!
//
// ALTERNATIVE: Use ConstrainedSymbolInGenerated with budget 60, which generates
// up to budget tokens and accepts the longest parser-valid prefix.
//
// BETTER ALTERNATIVE: Use CloseSpanWithinBudget from the START of the constrained
// span, but give it a hint by first generating some minimum context with
// AdaptiveConstrainedStep calls that do NOT check IsCompletePrefix, then
// use CloseSpanWithinBudget.
//
// ACTUAL FIX: The problem with CloseSpanWithinBudget is it closes at the FIRST
// complete prefix it sees. If we want to generate past complete prefixes, we
// need to run our own loop but NOT break on IsCompletePrefix until we have
// enough tokens.
//
// The key fix is: remove the !IsCompletePrefix guard from AppendConstrainedToken.
// But AppendConstrainedToken has that as a precondition...
//
// WORKAROUND: Use ConstrainedSymbolInGenerated with a budget of 60.
// It generates up to 60 tokens and accepts the LONGEST valid prefix.
// If the grammar allows extension past the first complete point, it will
// extend further. This naturally handles the "extend past first complete" issue.
//
// After ConstrainedSymbolInGenerated returns, we use CloseSpanWithinBudget
// to close cleanly.
//
// FINAL STRATEGY:
// 1. AppendTaskGuidance with clear expression guidance
// 2. UnconstrainedChunk for reasoning (up to 300 tokens)
// 3. Force open "<<" if not already open
// 4. ConstrainedSymbolInGenerated with budget 80 to generate the full expression
// 5. CloseSpanWithinBudget as safety net
